    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of different ways to distribute six identical copies of a book between the library and checked out, with the condition that at least one book is in the library and at least one is checked out, we can proceed as follows:\n\n1. **Total possible distributions**: First, calculate the total number of ways to distribute the six books between the library and checked out without any restrictions. Each book has 2 choices (either in the library or checked out), so there are \\(2^6 = 64\\) possible distributions.\n\n2. **Exclude invalid distributions**: We need to exclude the cases where all books are in the library or all books are checked out. There is exactly 1 way for all 6 books to be in the library, and there is exactly 1 way for all 6 books to be checked out. Therefore, we subtract these 2 invalid distributions from the total.\n\n3. **Calculate valid distributions**: The number of valid distributions is then \\(64 - 2 = 62\\).\n\nSo, the number of different ways to have some of the books in the library and the rest checked out, with at least one book in the library and at least one checked out, is \\(\\boxed{62}\\).",
